// Pathological witness: instantiating a dependent signature must not capture a
// caller's type variable.
//
// A dependent signature's parameters are binders. Instantiating one is a
// substitution INTO a scope that still binds the remaining ones, so a
// replacement mentioning a name that scope rebinds is captured unless the
// binder is renamed apart first. Shadowing and capture are different
// obligations and meeting the first does not meet the second.
//
// THE SEPARATING PAIR. The two callers below have the same shape, the same
// arity, and the same types. The only variable is whether the caller's binder
// names collide with the callee's. If instantiation captures, they disagree;
// if it is capture-avoiding, they agree. Nothing else distinguishes them, so a
// difference in outcome isolates capture and nothing else.
//
// WHY THIS SPELLING AND NOT A SMALLER ONE. Three type parameters is the least
// that exhibits it: the middle parameter's replacement must be a name a LATER
// parameter binds, so the sequence needs a before, a captured middle, and an
// after. A category's composition has exactly this shape, which is why every
// category law written the obvious way meets this and why the flagship's laws
// are spelled around it.
//
// THE SILENT HALF. Capture makes two types that should differ coincide as
// readily as it makes two that should agree diverge, so a refusal is the loud
// half of a fault whose other half accepts an argument written at the wrong
// type. A repair verified only against the refused program proves capture
// stopped rejecting something correct and says nothing about what it was
// accepting.

//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-def: collide
//@ expect-def: clean
//@ expect-type-without-unknown: collide
//@ expect-type-without-unknown: clean

// The callee. Its three type parameters are the binders instantiation must not
// capture into.
def comp(a : Type, b : Type, c : Type, f : U[ω] (a -> F b), g : U[ω] (b -> F c), x : a) -> F c {
  run y <- f(x);
  g(y)
}

// The colliding spelling. The caller names its own variables a b c d, so
// instantiating comp at (a, c, d) replaces comp's b by a name comp itself
// binds as c, and replaces comp's c by d.
def collide(a : Type, b : Type, c : Type, d : Type, u : U[ω] (a -> F c), h : U[ω] (c -> F d))
  -> F (U[ω] (a -> F d))
{
  ret thunk { comp(a, c, d, u, h) }
}

// The same body with the caller's variables renamed. No name the caller
// supplies is bound by the callee, so no binder can capture and this spelling
// is unaffected by the defect.
def clean(p : Type, q : Type, r : Type, s : Type, u : U[ω] (p -> F r), h : U[ω] (r -> F s))
  -> F (U[ω] (p -> F s))
{
  ret thunk { comp(p, r, s, u, h) }
}
